    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you expand more on the \"gates\" for the testing and staging parts? specifically what kind of failures or errors usually trigger a gate to close and prevent the build from progressing to the next stage? i want to make sure we have a clear list of what constitutes a \"fail\" for the team.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the testing stage, gates typically close if unit tests fail due to logic bugs, if code coverage falls below your minimum threshold (e.g., 85%), or if regression tests break, indicating existing features were accidentally broken. For the staging phase, gates will block promotion if security scanners detect critical vulnerabilities like SQL injection risks, if load tests show latency exceeding acceptable limits for high-volume transactions, or if compliance tools flag missing PII encryption.\n\nFurthermore, specific fintech failures include any API integration errors against mock partners or simulation of failed transaction rollbacks, which are common failure points for financial logic. Any linter violations regarding complex business rules in a fintech context should also trigger an immediate halt to prevent deploymenting potentially risky code. These specific triggers ensure that no code with known financial discrepancies or security holes ever moves toward production.",
      "source": "target",
